# ⚠️ ORDER MATTERS: git applies the LAST matching pattern, so the general rules come
# first and the hashed-file overrides come last. Putting them the other way round
# silently loses the override — which is exactly what happened on the first attempt,
# and a fresh clone came out with CRLF and hashes that no longer verified.

*.md    text
*.mjs   text
*.json  text

# ⛔ Hashed files: no EOL conversion, ever.
#
# manifest.json records a SHA-256 of each text.md. If git rewrites line endings on
# checkout, a clone produces different bytes than the recorded hash, and every
# acceptance record citing that hash stops verifying. `-text` makes the committed
# bytes the checked-out bytes on every platform. Do not "tidy" this to `text eol=lf`;
# that still normalises, and do not move these lines above the block above.
licenses/**/text.md   -text
manifest.json         -text
